.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 232 PM PST Fri Nov 20 2026
(This evening through Saturday night)

Scattered showers are making their way down the Central Coast with a
couple of rogue showers as far north as Alameda County. Satellite
imagery reveals an associated mesoscale low centered over the
Central Valley, close to Modesto. At the same time, relatively warm
ocean temperatures combined with cold air aloft are helping to keep
a modicum of instability and lift in the coastal waters, where a
couple cells showed enough rotation aloft to trigger Special Marine
Warnings across the waters off the Central Coast.

For the rest of the day, expect shower coverage to decrease across
Monterey and San Benito Counties as the band coming off that
mesoscale low moves southward, with widely isolated to isolated
showers elsewhere through the afternoon. Otherwise conditions remain
cold and mostly clear into the evening hours. High temperatures will
range from the middle to upper 50s in the lower elevations to the
middle 40s to lower 50s in the higher elevations. A Cold Weather
Advisory will once again go into effect at 1 AM through 8 AM
Saturday morning, with the inland North Bay, interior East Bay,
eastern Santa Clara Hills, the Santa Lucia mountains, the southern
Salinas Valley, and the interior Central Coast mountains seeing low
temperatures in the lower to middle 30s. A potential complication
will come from increasing cloud cover this evening and overnight,
especially in the North Bay, as the next system approaches the
region. If cloud cover comes in early enough overnight, low
temperatures could end up being noticeably warmer in the inland
valleys, possibly as warm as the low 40s. Incidentally, lows in
the lower to middle 40s are expected in areas close to the Bays
and the Pacific.

The second storm in a series of storm systems is expected to pass
through the region starting on Saturday. On the synoptic scale, this
storm will be the fringe impacts of a low pressure system swinging
down from the Gulf of Alaska before stalling out off the coast of
the Pacific Northwest, where the most rainfall will be targeted at
Oregon and the North Coast. By the time the rain band gets down to
our region, it will be fairly progressive. High resolution model
output depicts the rain arriving into the North Bay on Saturday
afternoon, moving into the SF Bay Area that evening, and coming into
the Central Coast overnight into Sunday morning. Post-frontal shower
activity may linger into the rest of Sunday. Rain totals will likely
be relatively light, especially compared to the last couple of days.
The current forecast calls for rain totals between 0.1" to 0.5",
with the higher totals expected along the coastal ranges and
especially within the Pacific coast of Sonoma County. Can not
discount the possibility of some stronger, convectively-driven,
showers on Sunday across the Central Coast and the coastal SF Bay
Area, which could significantly impact the final rain totals if a
particularly strong cell or a series of cells develops over a
particular area. Highs this weekend will be a little cooler across
the North Bay and similar to today`s everywhere else.

Coastal flooding will be a concern through the weekend and a major
concern into the next week. As it stands, a Coastal Flood Advisory
is in effect for the bayshore locations along the San Francisco and
San Pablo Bays through the high tide cycle on Saturday. The process
of extending the coastal flood products is underway, and will
include extending the Coastal Flood Advisory to the Pacific coast,
including Monterey Bay, and upgrading the Bays to a Coastal Flood
Warning. During the peak of the tide cycle from the 24th to the
26th, and perhaps for a couple days on either side of that period, 2
to 3 feet of coastal inundation is possible. Potential storm surge
and rainfall runoff can exacerbate the impacts. Now is the time to
prepare vulnerable properties. Turn around, don`t drown when
encountering flooded roadways!

