.LONG TERM...
Issued at 232 PM PST Fri Nov 20 2026
(Sunday through next Thursday)

Monday will represent a respite from the parade of storms, being
mainly dry if remaining a little cool with the inland valleys
hovering in the middle 50s to lower 60s. Use this day to prepare
your properties for the next storm, including clearing storm
drains, cleaning gutters, and making sure emergency kits are
stocked.

Another system comes into the region Tuesday into Wednesday, with
more significant rainfall and wind concerns than the weekend system.
A deep upper-level low sets up in the northern Pacific, setting up a
puff of moisture coming in from the subtropics. Widespread rainfall
is expected to arrive Tuesday morning and persist through the day
into the early part of Wednesday, with lingering showers possible
through Wednesday night. In addition, a strong pressure gradient
between the low in the northern Pacific and high pressure over the
Rocky Mountains will result in strong winds on Tuesday and early
Wednesday. Widespread Wind Advisory criteria gusts are not expected
at this time, but gusts of 40 to 50 mph are possible along the
coast, through favored gaps and passes, and within the Salinas
Valley. As a final note, this system is also impacting during the
King Tide cycle, and any rain runoff can exacerbate coastal
flooding impacts, and impacts the Thanksgiving travel period.

The storm door remains open into the end of November. Longer range
models indicate a potential system around the last weekend of
November.

