.BEACHES...
Issued at 151 PM PST Thu Nov 19 2026

For the High Surf Advisory:

A High Surf Advisory remains in effect through 4 AM Friday with
breaking waves of 18 to 24 ft with up to 30 ft and favored break
points. The risk of sneaker waves and strong rip currents will
persist as well. These waves are less energetic than the ones we saw
on this past Saturday, which ended up flooding portions of Pacifica
and damaging Capitola Pier, but remain hazardous to potential
swimmers, could still knock unaware people into the water, and
continue to feed an increased risk of rip currents. The risk of
drowning remains high. Do not go in the water, avoid climbing on the
rocks or leaning over seawalls, and never turn your back on the
ocean.

For the Coastal Flood Advisory:

A combination of high astronomical tides and thermal expansion from
warmer than normal waters will result in tidal flooding concerns
across Bayshore locations in the San Francisco Bay and San Pablo
Bay. At the San Francisco tidal gauge, high tide is expected to be
1.2 ft above normal (7.0 ft MLLW) at 7:15 AM Thursday and 1.1 ft
above normal (6.9 ft MLLW) at 7:45 AM Friday. These predictions
include up to 1.8 ft of surge that will enhance the astronomical
tide and flooding threat. High tide varies up to 90 minutes earlier
or later along the Pacific Coast and through the San Francisco Bay,
respectively.

The Pacific Coast will likely be added to the Advisory as early as
Sunday with the San Francisco and San Pablo Bays upgraded to a
Coastal Flood Warning as we approach "King Tides" November 24-26.

